 thinking  so depending on when you come in you may  miss some of these other symptoms and  not get diagnosed  so sue what is it exactly and what is it  that you think we most don't understand  about it  i think it's the unpredictability it's  hard for doctors to recognize because  when you go into a primary care visit or  even a trained mental health  professional  they're seeing you at one snapshot in  time and what they really need to do is  take a longitudinal history and  ask family members and ask them does  this person have  difficulty falling asleep or do they are  they are able to function on very little  sleep  do they make a lot of impulsive plans do  they plan to like you know go from one  city to another are they going to vegas  when you know we have that freedom are  they buying shirts in five different  colors  again these are very stereotypical  things but are important to ask because  they often get  missed the impairment in functioning is  very severe  people don't realize that bipolar  disorder often and a lot of mental  health conditions  when they're not treated become chronic  and even though i'm  very much of the belief of holistic  lifestyle intervention such as talk  therapy exercise nutrition  there really is a role of medication in  this and a person may not  need may need to be on it for an  extended period of time and that can be  hard for a person to swallow  especially when they're in the midst of  a manic episode or a depression  they might be feeling paranoid and they  think that maybe someone's poisoned them  maybe they don't  someone is out to get them so the  hardest thing about this is that a  patient's own  insight and awareness and judgment are  affected by the illness  you know a lot of the chatter around it  was why don't people get help why don't  family members get help why don't people  intervene  and in her letter kim pointed out you  know that's very difficult unless the  individuals themselves  want to engage in the process of getting  help so what can family members do  what can loved ones do in situations  like these  you know be very involved in the  person's life  be vigilant get educated about it  if and when they're willing because  there might be narrow windows of  opportunity where somebody may say  i don't feel well can you help me they  may not even ask for your help but they  might just appreciate your support so  i'm a big believer in family therapy  it's something i do in my practice  and even if i'm just treating the  individual i will try to engage the  family time to time  the family members must be involved you  know look it takes a village to raise  any person a healthy person a sp but  especially someone who's dealing with a  condition or a disorder we really need  to rally against the person  what makes it hard is because often some  of these behaviors because they're  erratic  family members might feel alienated they  might feel pushed away  they might feel scared by the behavior  it's unpredictable they don't know what  to expect  a person might lash out and be angry at  them so i would say if you can  understand this as a brain illness  not take it personally and say listen  in what capacity can i be helpful do you  want me to drive you to your doctor's  appointment do you want me to find you a  doctor i'll wait in the parking lot i  don't need to be a part of it  but if they're open to and if they give  you permission  try to have a conversation if you're a  close family member to say let me  understand  what are the symptoms and how can i help  them what are the medications what are  the side effects  side effects of medication are a big  reason why people get off of  a get off of them and then relapse  yeah i think what you said sue was very  important think of this as a brain  illness  i mean in in our ongoing effort to  reduce the stigma and the shame  what's the best thing you can say about  that part of this conversation  this needs to be taken very seriously in  the pandemic  all of us are experiencing heightened  stress you are not alone  please don't feel isolated there is  absolutely no shame  no stigma you're not less of a person  you didn't cause this to yourself  please get help help is available take  your time do your due diligence find a  trusted person  and give the treatment some time it  always takes time to work  all right dr sue varma always good to  see you thank you very much  if you or someone you know is seeking  mental health resources you can call the  national alliance on mental illness  helpline  that number is 1-800-950-6264  i know it's hard to write it down  quickly so we'll have it on our website  or in a crisis you can text nami to  741-741
 

